  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded user-adopted interpretation, the translated rectangle-conservation hypothesis identifies the intended integral solutions. The conclusion supplies precisely the interval mass-rate law almost everywhere for each fixed interval and rules out spatial differentiability at each specified spatial discontinuity, hence classical solutionhood under that convention. The separate existential conjunct confirms that the discontinuous solution class is nonempty. This implication concerns the selected classical-versus-integral assertion, not the neighboring claim about formation from smooth data."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded user-adopted interpretation, integral solutionhood supplies the stated rectangle balance and integrability, with the mass-rate identity almost everywhere separately for each interval. A spatial discontinuity excludes every spatial derivative and thus excludes the translated classical conjunction for every operator assignment, independently of its relation to the flux. Nonemptiness is compatible with the source's discontinuous-solution assertion and is concretely witnessed by a stationary scalar step with zero flux at any positive time. Oriented intervals, finite-coordinate representation, and the vacuous zero-dimensional case introduce no substantive additional requirement."
